The 97% consensus figure has been independently replicated at least five times using different methodologies — Cook et al. (2013), Anderegg et al. (2010), Oreskes (2004), Carlton et al. (2015), and Lynas et al. (2021).
Climate models built in the 1980s predicted the warming pattern we observe today with remarkable accuracy — this is the gold standard test of a scientific theory.
The isotopic signature of atmospheric CO₂ (depleted in C-14 and C-13) is a chemical fingerprint that unambiguously identifies fossil fuel burning as the source of the increase.
Natural factors — solar variability, volcanic eruptions, ocean cycles — have been measured and cannot account for the observed warming trend since 1950.
Insurance companies, reinsurers like Swiss Re and Munich Re, price climate risk into their models — they have strong financial incentive to get this right and they treat anthropogenic climate change as established fact.
